              "Sing a song: “What is missing?”\n*Aims: to create a friendly and active atmosphere in the class to beginning the lesson.\n*Procedure:\n- Divide class into 4 teams.\n- Set rules: Look at the pictures and find out what is missing (kite, car, plane, train, truck, ship, bus, teddy bear)\n+ Invite Ss to find out which one is missing. \n+ The fastest have correct answer get points for the team.\n- Encourage Ss to join in the game.\n- Give point for Ss.\n- Say “Open your book page 43” and look at “Unit 17, Lesson 2 (4,5,6)”.\n\n2.Practice: (27’)\nActivity 1.   Listen and tick:\n*Aims: Ss will be able to listen to and understand two communicative contexts in which pupils talk about the quantity of toys that some people have and tick the correct pictures.\n*Procedure:\n-   Elicit the quantity of toys that the boys have in each  picture \n\n- T ask Ss to guess the answers.\n+ T writes Ss’guessing on the board.\n- Listen to the tape:\n+  Play the recording\n+ Play the recording again and get Ss to swap books with their partners.\n+  Play the recording again to check answers together as a class. \n+ Write the answers on the board for Ss to correct their answers. Then checks Ss’ guessing.\n-  Play the recording, sentence by sentence, for the class to listen and repeat in chorus. \nKey: 1.b    2. a\n\nActivity 2.  Look, complete and read.\n*Aims: Ss will be able to complete four sentences with the help of picture cues.\n*Procedure:\n-   Have Ss look at the pictures and identify the quantity of toys in the picture.\n\n- T models with sentence 1. \n+ Have Ss look at the sentence. \n+ Ask them what is missing in the answer (buses). \n+ Have Ss complete the gap.\n+ Have Ss read the completed sentence in chorus.\n-Have Ss work in pairs and complete the sentences 2, 3 and 4.\n-  Invite pairs of pupils to stand up and read aloud the sentences.\n- T gives feedbacks.\nKey: 1. buses   2. two ships\n 3. three dolls 4. have two teddy bears\n\nActivity 3.    Let’s play.\n*Aims: Ss will be able to review vocabulary words for toys by playing Kim’s game.\n*Procedure:\n- Elicit the language that pupils need to name the toys.\n\n- Tell the Ss the goal of the game and how to play it: Ss should look at the cards carefully and recall their names and positions successfully. \n- Divide the class into two teams. \n- Display both sets of cards face up on the board. Give Ss two minutes to memorise their set. Turn the cards face down on the board. \n+ Explain that when you say a word, a nominated pupil from each team must run to their set, try to remember which card shows the relevant picture and turn it over to show to their team. \n+ A correct card scores one point. When the Ss’ turn is over, they must put the cards back on the board and return to their seats. \n+ Remember to nominate a pupil from each team before saying the word. \n- Count the points for each team and announce the winner. \n+ The team with the most points wins. \n+ The winning team must then correctly pronounce all of the words.\n\n*Consolidation\n*Aims: Ss will be able consolidate and wrap up the content of the lesson.\n*Procedure:\n- T asks ss to answer the following questions:\n  + What have you learnt from the lesson today? \n+What are the core values of the lesson? (if the teacher can take from the lesson).\n- T praises some Ss who are hardworking / active/ good…. as well as encourage others to try more.\n\n\n3. Homework. (3’)\n- Prepare the new lesson: Unit 17, Lesson 3 (1,2,3).\nDigital & AI mini-task: Review detective\n- T displays 2–3 short AI-style review answers, including at least one deliberate error.\n- T asks pupils to compare each answer with the textbook, picture or learnt sentence pattern and correct the error.\n- Evidence: pupil identifies and corrects at least one inaccurate AI-style answer.",
